WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.220
- Hey, Addy Hour fam.

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:03.210
Just want to give a little
bit of an introduction

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.760
to this episode 29,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.840
this conversation with
Rabbi Angela Buchdahl,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.380
we're actually releasing on
different a day this week,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.970
just with everything that
we've experienced as a society.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:16.490
I'm recording this intro
on the 26th of May,

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.080
and I debated for a while,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.970
on whether to release
the episode this week,

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.230
where to give some time
just as we've continued

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.880
to experience tragedy after tragedy

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.130
with these shootings first, not first,

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.410
but recently in Buffalo,
then again in California,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.600
in Texas in elementary school.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.530
And so I debated whether
to release this episode

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.310
because some of the
topics that we brought up

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.340
were also related to that in the sense

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.580
that Rabbi Buchdahl also
walked or has walked through

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.550
and helped navigate a hostage situation

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.530
at synagogue in Texas in January.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:48.730
And that's something that she talked about

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:49.970
on the episode as well.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.070
So I just wanted to be sensitive
to where you would be all

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.970
or were as a society, where
you all were as listeners,

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.830
but ultimately I thought it
would still be good to release

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:59.850
the episode because there's
so many aspects of hope

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.880
that she also shared in this episode

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.710
in terms of just things
that she has been navigating

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.070
as a leader, things that she
has navigated in her synagogue

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.450
and in her Jewish community

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.600
and I think important lessons for us all.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.380
So again, that's the reason
that we decided to wait

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.670
and not release this episode

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.930
the day after the tragic shooting.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:18.780
Our hearts go out to all those families,

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.870
not even being able to
imagine what it's like

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.490
to lose a child in that type of setting

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.190
on such an unfortunate circumstance.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.260
And in so many ways,

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.950
obviously there are lots
of policy implications

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.230
so we could actually do better

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.680
to try and prevent these
things from happening.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.340
So with that being said,
just wanted to make sure

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.720
that I put this in context.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.930
I hope that you all
still will take the time

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:42.230
to process and to mourn and
to grieve as you need to,

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.550
but also to know that we
are here for each other

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.170
and we need to support
each other in this time.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.320
And I think that's something
you'll definitely get

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.637
in this episode as well.

00:01:49.637 --> 00:01:51.530
But again, just wanted
to put things in context

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.197
and to let you all know about that,

00:01:53.197 --> 00:01:55.440
but I encourage you to
listen to the episode.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.630
So many important things
that we were able to discuss,

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.620
and I believe that
you'll find it cathartic

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.570
and healing in some ways as well.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.120
And I appreciate you
all, always being here

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.593
as an Addy Hour family and for everything

00:02:05.593 --> 00:02:08.622
that you all do to invest in this podcast.

00:02:08.622 --> 00:02:11.205
(upbeat music)

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.560
Well, welcome to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.680
I'm grateful to continue to
host these conversations.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:21.990
I will say on the front end,

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.450
we are operating a spirit
of flexibility today

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.700
as we've been navigating
some technical challenges,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.710
but again, it's so important
for us to just be flexible

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.770
in this moment and something
that I've been saying

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:31.603
to a lot of people.

00:02:31.603 --> 00:02:33.240
So it makes sense that
I would have to move

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.960
in that space as well.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.990
But again, grateful that
you all continue to join

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.780
these conversations, actually recording

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.680
in the Yale podcast studio again today.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.040
So thankful for the team
and for all they've done

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.590
to really just help us
facilitate these conversations

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.570
over the last year and a half.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.640
Today, I'm honored to be able to welcome

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.980
Dr. oh my goodness, rabbi,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:52.813
that's something I've done--
- You elevated me.

00:02:52.813 --> 00:02:54.450
- I've done to multiple guests.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.747
You're not the first,

00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:59.020
but to introduce Rabbi Angela Buchdahl,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.360
who is here joining us
for a conversation today.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.130
So grateful to have you
here on the podcast.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.210
- Grateful to be here.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.670
- And for today's conversation,

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.150
it's just gonna be an open conversation

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.160
on a lot of different topics.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.540
I haven't assigned a
specific title or theme,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.470
which is a little bit
different for us, but again,

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.500
I think as you all listen
into this conversation,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.660
you'll see why that's the
case because there's so many

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.220
important things that we may
touch on as we go through.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.270
So just by way of introduction
for any who aren't familiar

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.440
or familiar with the Rabbi,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.010
I just wanted to mention
her background a little bit.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.630
She serves as a Senior Rabbi

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.950
of Central Synagogue in New York City

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:38.950
and is the first woman to lead
Central's Reform Congregation

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.160
in its 180 year history.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.640
So Rabbi Buchdahl first
joined Central Synagogue

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.290
as a Senior Cantor in 2006, and in 2014,

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.973
she was chosen by the congregation
to be the senior rabbi.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.710
She was born in Korea to
a Jewish American father

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.210
and a Korean Buddhist mother.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.470
Later in life, she attended and earned

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:03.380
a Bachelor's Degree in
Arts and Religious Studies

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:07.140
from Yale University here on campus.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.090
And of course later became ordained

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.370
as a first Asian American to be ordained

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.140
as a Cantor or rabbi in North America.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.430
So someone who is a
pioneer and a trailblazer

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.220
in a lot of ways.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.430
She's been nationally recognized
in a lot of different ways

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.320
and also for her innovations
in leading worship,

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.770
she's been featured in
dozens of news outlets,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.510
including The Today Show, NPR
and PBS, and was also listed

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:34.510
as one of Newsweek's America's
50 Most Influential Rabbis.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.010
Angela is someone who also serves

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.870
on a lot of different organizations,
and boards, on the AGC,

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.930
also on the New York Board of Rabbis,

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.520
on the UJA Federation of New York

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.670
and on the Yale University
President's Council.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.010
She's married to her husband, Jacob,

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.620
and they have three children.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.657
So again, someone that
I've been introduced to

00:04:52.657 --> 00:04:54.220
and have had the pleasure of being able

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.210
to interact with a little bit
and really looking forward

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.700
to the conversation this afternoon.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.330
So thanks so much for being here.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:04.160
- Me too, you left out that
I was the college roommate

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.140
of Dr. Esther Choo, who
was our point of connection

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.572
to begin with, and my claim
to fame in the doctor award.

00:05:10.572 --> 00:05:11.820
(Addy laughs)

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.011
- Maybe that's where the
doctor part slipped for me.

00:05:14.011 --> 00:05:14.844
- Maybe that's what it was.

00:05:14.844 --> 00:05:17.470
- Perhaps, I'm elevating you, but I think,

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.870
you know, humor aside,
there are so many things

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.610
that you have so much influence
and impact in as well.

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:24.020
So who knows, maybe that's something

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.390
that will come as an honorary
at some point in the future,

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.560
but I'm starting that today, but yes,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.120
definitely appreciate that
recognition of Dr. Choo

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.380
connecting the two of us and
appreciate her investment

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.030
in what we're doing here as well.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:38.910
So again, just to start out, as you know

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.870
and as my listeners know,
I think it's so important

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.900
for us to have check-ins with each other,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.040
as we engage in some
of these conversations.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.740
And especially as we're
recording at this point in time

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.580
in the middle of May,
the third week of May.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.210
Obviously this has been
another challenging week

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.047
for our country, even
as we have been mourning

00:05:55.047 --> 00:05:58.050
the loss of so many
lives, first in Buffalo

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:01.110
with a tragic situation
there, also in California.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:03.320
And also just acknowledging
that a lot of these things

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.830
are ongoing, even events
that don't necessarily

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.890
hit the national news media in a sense.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:12.200
But at the same time, we have
a lot of room also for joy,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.890
as we see people collectively
supporting each other

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.810
in so many different ways,
as we have reasons for hope.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.260
And so with those two things in mind,

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.930
just wanted to check in with
you and see how you are doing

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.080
at this point in time with everything

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.373
that we're navigating
through as a society.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.140
- I think I'm feeling what a lot of people

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:34.350
in this country are feeling,
which is a mix of despair

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:39.350
and deep upset and hurt over recent

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.823
devastating gun violence,
racial hatred that we're seeing.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:47.980
Last two weeks ago, the news
about the leaked decision

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:49.730
about abortion Supreme Court

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.630
and where we might be
heading with women's rights.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:56.170
And there's a lot that we
are very concerned about.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:59.020
And I think that for me
as a person who works

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:04.020
in the religious field, I think
that spiritual resilience,

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:07.780
like part of the work now
is that we actually keep up

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.920
our sense of spiritual
resilience to have the will

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.150
to not give into that
despair, to actually believe

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.970
and remind ourselves that
change is always happening

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.900
and continues to be possible,
change for the better.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:24.640
And I think that that's
where we are right now,

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.410
and I actually have to
keep reminding myself

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.270
of that message at times.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.620
- Really well said and I
take that to heart as well,

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.740
even in terms of the reminders

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.490
that we have to give ourselves.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:37.810
How would you say that
those in your congregation

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.520
are also navigating
through all this as well

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.610
in terms of what you've seen in them,

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.560
and then also in your role as a leader?

00:07:45.894 --> 00:07:49.830
- I think people are experiencing
this on different levels.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.280
Some are taking it very personally,

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.420
some are experiencing it
with a great deal of fear

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.450
and anxiety, and there are others

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.264
that have kind of built up a wall

00:07:58.264 --> 00:08:02.990
and actually have stopped
listening to the news,

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.720
stopped actually caring.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.868
I don't know, actually
what is more frightening.

00:08:06.868 --> 00:08:09.130
We often talk about the
fact that indifference

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:13.190
is one of the gravest sins,
'cause it sort of enables

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:14.540
and allows all of this to happen.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:18.750
So I don't think there's just
one response that I'm seeing,

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:23.190
and I think there's a
certain kind of process

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.530
that people go through when
they're grieving something

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:28.830
or experiencing a trauma of some kind,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.902
that some of these things
are all that for people

00:08:31.902 --> 00:08:34.610
and you have to help
people move through it

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.360
to a place that is with a
little bit more healing,

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.960
a little more productive,
a little more hopeful.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.488
So I think that's the task.

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:44.260
- And I appreciate how
sensitive it sounds like you are

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:47.300
to just all the different
places that people are

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.860
and even, to take it a step
further, I think for many of us,

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.630
we're also in a lot of
those different places

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:52.650
at different times.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:55.053
So, at one moment it might be for me,

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.610
walking through morning at another moment,

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.090
outrage and frustration,
sometimes some despair as well,

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.660
like, oh, this is happening again,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:04.980
are things ever going to get better.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:06.737
And just knowing that
there needs to be time

00:09:06.737 --> 00:09:08.440
and place for all those pieces,

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.400
and it sounds like you're
really empathetic to that

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:12.760
and cognizant of that

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:15.740
as you're in such an important
leadership role as well.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.450
So that's definitely
something I appreciate

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.210
and I imagine that those in
your synagogue would appreciate

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.660
that level of empathy
and awareness as well.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.100
So I just wanted to acknowledge that.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:25.933
- Thank you.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.900
- So obviously a lot that we're
walking through and again,

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.120
just in your role as a leader,

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:33.720
a lot that you also often
have to carry with you,

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:36.180
yourself and as you're
leading others as well.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:37.777
So I know we'll come back
to that in the conversation,

00:09:37.777 --> 00:09:40.420
but I also wanted to take a
step back a little bit as well,

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.650
because as I was just
mentioning your introduction

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.830
and your path, there's a lot there,

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:47.850
there's a lot of important
components there,

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.600
there's a lot of trail blazing there.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.720
And I was just curious if
you'd be willing to share

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.930
with our listeners a
little bit about your path

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.390
and your journey from growing up

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:00.880
to becoming in this
esteemed role and someone

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.930
who is pioneering in
so many different ways,

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.053
how did that journey come about for you?

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.310
- Well, so much of this
with so much of life

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:16.310
is a product of some
fortunate timing and luck

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:22.573
as well as just I think
people following a calling

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:24.860
if they're able to listen

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.670
to what they're sort of called to do.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:30.850
And so I certainly never
set out as a little kid

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.540
to think to myself, oh, I
wanna be a first of anything

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.023
honestly, or a trailblazer.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.200
What I was doing was sort of
listening to a call that I had

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.770
from a pretty young age,
that I was very attuned

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.747
to sort of the spirit of the universe.

00:10:43.747 --> 00:10:48.747
And I received in many ways,
my spiritual sensitivity

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:53.590
from my Buddhist mother,
but then I received

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:55.870
a sort of spiritual and ritual vocabulary

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.050
from my Jewish father
with the Jewish community

00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.420
in which I was raised.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:03.540
So I think that, actually many Jews

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:07.000
are not actually that
comfortable talking about God,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:07.960
it might surprise you.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:09.920
It's not like many other faith traditions

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.860
in which people very
comfortably talk about it.

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:13.160
But the vast majority of Jews

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.920
that are in sort of progressive circles,

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.360
at best you could say many of them

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:19.010
are sort of uncomfortable
talking about it.

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:22.250
But so I think my mother
gave me a sense of comfort,

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:23.370
not just talking about God,

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.620
but actually being in
conversation with God

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.510
on a regular basis.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:32.510
And then I think, but the fact
that my father and my mother

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.620
decided that my sister
and I would be raised

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:35.990
within the Jewish community,

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.210
gave us sort of a ritual
yearly structure for holidays

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:45.210
and Shabbat gatherings and
meals and biblical stories

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:47.400
that sort of informed the way

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:48.420
that I walked through the world.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:51.580
So in some ways, a
foundation was set early.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:56.580
I'm not a typical path to the
rabbinate as like an immigrant

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.180
from South Korea who comes to America,

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:02.260
grows up in a tiny Jewish
community in Tacoma, Washington,

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:04.747
and goes on to lead a large
synagogue in New York.

00:12:04.747 --> 00:12:06.270
- Very true (laughing).

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:09.320
- That is an unusual
story, but in many ways,

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:12.810
part of the luck of this
is that I was born in 1972,

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:15.650
which was the year that
the very first female rabbi

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:18.240
was ordained, so it's 50 years this year

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.070
that the first woman was ordained,

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:23.190
which in the scope of 3,000
years of Jewish history,

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:24.810
that's just a blink.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:29.500
And at the same time, there
was not a day of my lifetime

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.100
that I didn't think it was possible

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:32.170
for a woman to be a rabbi.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.300
So in that way, I was very lucky.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.810
I was also lucky my parents
were married in 1969,

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.110
this was just a year after
Loving versus Virginia,

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:43.600
which meant that interracial
marriage was not outlawed

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.310
in some of our states in America.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:49.170
So here, my parents
were only a year later,

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.460
were getting married at a time

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.600
when people were still getting accustomed

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.660
to the idea of interracial marriages.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.530
And I was fortunate that
their love was not illegal

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:02.000
in any state, by the time they
got married, but only barely.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.420
I was lucky that I was born
at a time when Title IX passed

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:09.060
and it took a place like Yale,

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:11.590
which had only just
right before I was born

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:14.070
a few years before, started to take women,

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.340
but for a while, was still
only wanting to keep women

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:20.920
to about 15% of the college
until Title IX came around

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:23.670
and then they were forced to
have to actually go 50/50.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.720
They did not want to go there willingly.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.670
So I think about these kinds
of things that all contributed

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.510
to this path that
ultimately I got to be on.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.230
And I recognize how many
shoulders I'm standing on

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:38.000
in order to have been able to be a woman

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.150
who could become a rabbi, who
could have a Korean mother,

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:41.940
who could end up leading a community.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.570
And the last thing I'd say
is that the Reform Movement,

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:47.673
which is the progressive
movement of the Jewish community,

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:50.113
made a decision in the late '70s

00:13:50.113 --> 00:13:51.960
that they were actually gonna be welcoming

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:53.170
of interfaith families.

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.520
For a long time, the policy,
because it's a community

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:00.150
that really values endogamy
and Jews marrying Jews

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:01.803
for Jewish continuity sake.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:07.540
Its policy used to be,
we're gonna reject anyone

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:09.340
who marries outside of the faith.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:10.690
If you ever saw "Fiddler on the Roof,"

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.840
which is a lot of people's
Jewish introduction,

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:15.450
they literally shiver.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:18.210
They say the death prayer

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.423
for the person who has married out.

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:25.400
It was a controversial
and very brave decision

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:28.100
that the movement took in the '70s to say,

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.640
actually, this is not good
for Jewish continuity,

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.160
what's gonna be good for our community

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.990
is for us to welcome those who marry out.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:39.770
So I was also coming to
America just at a time

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:41.600
when the Jewish community's
attitudes were changing

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.310
towards families, just like mine.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.400
So all of these things
contribute to how I got to be

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:50.250
where I am and it's a confluence
of gender equality issues

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:53.750
that were just coming of
age, racial acceptance

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:57.860
and interfaith and inter-religious
and interracial couples

00:14:57.860 --> 00:15:01.250
that were just becoming
acceptable in society,

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:04.670
just as I was being born into a family,

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:05.980
there was all of those things.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:10.980
So, I recognize that if I'd
been born a generation earlier

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:13.480
or even 10 years earlier,

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.660
my life could not be
possible as it unfolded.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:16.910
So I think about that a lot.

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:19.660
And frankly, because
I'm a person of faith,

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:23.990
I think that there was a sense
of, this is why I'm called

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:27.220
to be in this place right
now, because I could,

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.930
and because I think the
generations before me

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:29.880
probably could not.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.262
- Wow, I mean, that's such
a helpful perspective,

00:15:33.262 --> 00:15:36.966
as I'm listening to share
all those different aspects,

00:15:36.966 --> 00:15:39.810
some may say serendipity,
but I mean, there's so many

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.840
that you just listed in terms of timing,

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.370
had things been not even the generation,

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:45.203
but five years earlier,

00:15:45.203 --> 00:15:47.260
just so many pieces that were in place.

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:50.750
So it's just, I think it's
actually very encouraging

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.900
for me to hear how front
and center that perspective

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:55.000
seems to be for you.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.030
I mean, it doesn't sound like
in any sense it's forgotten

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:58.620
or you had to dig deep to pull it back up,

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:00.350
but it's there kind of
on the tip of your tongue

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:02.740
and that you know
exactly how each of these

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:05.730
different perspectives
really paved the way.

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:09.630
- I will be honest with
you, when I got to Yale

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:13.680
in the '90s, I would think of myself

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:15.290
not as like a reluctant feminist

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:16.830
or I didn't quite understand,

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.630
why do we still have a women's center?

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.970
Like the job of feminism is done.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.520
I feel so naive thinking
about myself that way,

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:25.890
but that was sort of the way I felt then,

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.290
because I felt like I could
do anything a man could do.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:30.950
It wasn't until I got
actually a little bit older

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:33.140
before I started having
kids, hit the glass ceiling

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:36.420
for the first time, that
I actually appreciated

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.670
that actually, oh, we still
have so much work to do.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.983
In fact, if anything, I became
the angry feminist in my 40s.

00:16:41.983 --> 00:16:43.080
- Wow.

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.100
- But I would say the reason in part

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:46.600
that I'm able to reflect on this now

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:48.700
is actually I'm working on a memoir,

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:51.650
and it's funny how you
look back on these things

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:53.767
and suddenly all those pieces
kind of came into place.

00:16:53.767 --> 00:16:56.670
And I don't think it took
some maturity, honestly

00:16:56.670 --> 00:17:00.390
and some retrospection to actually think

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:03.360
about how many sort of social movements

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:07.860
had to lay the foundation
for where I could be.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.810
And it made me appreciate
that fight so much more.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:12.100
It made me realize, first of all,

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:14.780
that we're obviously not
done with any of those fights

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:19.200
from feminism to racial justice,
to acceptance across lines

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:20.590
of difference and all of those things,

00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:23.360
but it made me appreciate
that first of all,

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:25.670
we've made some progress, even
though we have work to do.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:29.730
And it made me reflect on
it in a way that I actually,

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:33.530
frankly, when I was younger,
didn't appreciate honestly.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:34.817
- Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:17:34.817 --> 00:17:36.960
And I appreciate your
honesty and giving us

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:39.440
that perspective as well, which
this is gonna sound funny,

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:41.970
but I think is hopeful for many
of us as well to feel like,

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:43.190
well, there's always room for growth,

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:45.880
even as you've stated that so importantly,

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:47.500
but some of the things that
you may not have realized

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:50.050
and had to look back on are now so fresh

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:51.570
and clear to you as well.

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:52.440
And again, just in your story,

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.850
I mean, there's so many important
elements that are there,

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:56.990
even from the family
dynamic and the empowerment

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.460
you had there to some of
the things and movements

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.410
that were happening, but I'm also thinking

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:03.950
just in terms of the importance of policy.

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:05.630
I mean, a lot of the
things that you referenced

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:08.810
were policies that were
changed that had direct impact

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:11.970
on your ability to move
forward in certain circles.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:14.550
That's something that comes
up in so many different ways.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.410
I mean, I can think about
that on the research side

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.140
with some of the work that
we're doing, for instance,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:22.960
around e-cigarettes and how
policy can influence and impact

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:26.230
aspects of health when it comes to youth

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:29.010
who maybe using e-cigarettes,
young people or adults

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:30.880
and all those types of processes.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.370
But even as you mentioned
around all the diversity,

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.660
equity, inclusion,
anti-racism, how the policy

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:38.720
can have direct impacts on people's lives.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:39.700
I think in a lot of different ways,

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:40.907
sometimes we like to acknowledge that

00:18:40.907 --> 00:18:43.210
and sometimes it seems like we pretend

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:44.690
that that's not the case,

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:47.760
that these things are just
philosophical conversations

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.600
when there's so much more to it as well.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:51.260
So I mean, so much of what you said

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:52.967
is resonating with me as well

00:18:52.967 --> 00:18:55.270
and I'm attempting not
to become too tangential,

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:56.820
but I think it's just, I
mean, everything you share

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:00.660
is such a concrete example of
so many ways that has happened

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:03.450
in your life that has been
beneficial to you as a person,

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.390
as an individual who has
grown and has also allowed you

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:08.830
to have so much impact
in so many ways as well.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.150
So I'm just reflecting as you're talking

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.520
and kind of piecing all
those things together

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:15.800
and appreciative again, of
the way that you've been able

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:18.380
to just describe that and
already looking forward

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:19.859
to whatever this memoir is,

00:19:19.859 --> 00:19:21.490
that you've just referenced as well.

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.770
- Well, unfortunately my day job keeps me

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:25.387
from having a lot of time to write,

00:19:25.387 --> 00:19:27.690
but I've done a little more
reflection, so that's good.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:31.490
- Yeah, speaking of the day
job, I'm also just curious,

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:33.360
as you've taken us on
this journey a little bit,

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.220
how do people respond to your journey,

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:38.340
both within the Jewish community,

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:41.120
but the community at large,
because I can anticipate

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.730
and imagine that were different responses

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:45.830
and reactions in different circles.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:47.390
And I'm just curious what that was like

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:48.833
from your perspective.

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:54.760
- I think that you have to
have a sense of humor about it.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:58.010
And I think that when
I was younger, I mean,

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:00.060
there wasn't a single time I could walk

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:02.560
into a new Jewish community

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.747
and not get lots and lots of questions

00:20:04.747 --> 00:20:08.810
and they could come from
places of genuine curiosity

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:12.080
and welcome, and they could sometimes come

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:14.600
from a place of challenge and ignorance

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:18.750
and sometimes mean spiritedness.

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:22.300
And I think the question
that I got was some variation

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:23.820
of, that's funny, you don't look Jewish,

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:28.240
or, how are you possibly
Jewish or whatever it was.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:33.240
And none of those things feel
like massively threatening

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:37.340
in any sort of single instance.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.230
But if it's sort of your experience,

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:41.690
every time you walk into a community,

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.340
you feel constantly othered,

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:46.730
you feel kind of constantly
like you don't really belong

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:47.810
and like a stranger.

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:50.150
And so there was a certain aspect of that,

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:52.620
that was a defining
feature of what it was like

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.833
to walk into a new Jewish community.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.400
That being said, once I was in a community

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:02.310
like my home community in
Tacoma, it didn't take long

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:07.310
before people just saw
me as Angela and as a Jew

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:12.160
and as part of the
community and they were open

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.170
to what I could contribute

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:15.660
and how I wanted to be a part of it.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.650
And so, I mean, I think that I obviously

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:20.690
couldn't be a rabbi today if
I didn't get a lot of support

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:24.870
and love and sense of meaning
from people along the way,

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:28.600
mentors, Jewish community
members, like my surrogate aunties

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:33.490
and uncles, and my congregations
who really welcomed me

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:34.740
and were extremely loving.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:36.700
So, I think that the good news

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:41.610
is you get past that sort of
initial, how are you Jewish?

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:45.130
And then you just establish
yourself in a way.

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:48.200
And so I would say, I'm
now at a stage in my career

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.190
where people know who I am

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.600
and I don't get the same questions.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:53.610
And I think I mistakenly
kind of felt like,

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.780
okay, well, I'm the senior rabbi

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:57.510
of a large Manhattan congregation

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.280
with a lot of Jews of color
within my congregation.

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:02.720
And I thought, okay, well,
since I'm here at the front,

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:04.560
things are all better, right?

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:07.990
Well recently in light of
the George Floyd murder

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:10.010
and just many more conversations,

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.260
sort of a national reckoning
that we were all having

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.210
as a country around race,
people in my community

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:17.300
came out of the woodwork
and shared their own stories

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:19.760
of racism within the Jewish community.

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:24.760
And I think I didn't appreciate
that their experience

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:27.660
was still so much more
like mine was 20 years ago

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:31.730
and it wasn't enough that
I was the senior rabbi.

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:35.710
There was still ways that our
community was not responding

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:36.910
as I kind of had hoped.

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:41.330
And so I think, I ended up
giving a sermon about this.

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.220
I knew that at kind of the
most Holy Day of the Year

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.243
when I have the most people
watching on Yom Kippur,

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:50.840
where I have thousands in
my congregation watching,

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:53.780
and then hundreds of thousands
watching on live stream,

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:56.570
I knew I wanted to address
racism in our country

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:58.920
coming off of the summer that we'd had

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:00.740
when George Floyd was murdered.

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:02.650
I didn't realize until
I started to sit down

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:04.820
and write the sermon that
ultimately I had to start

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:07.890
actually with what I
understood was the racism

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:09.610
that was still existent
within our community.

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.460
I felt like you have to
start with your own house.

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:14.670
And our own house still had work to do.

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.600
And I recognize that this is a muscle

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:18.800
that you have to actually exercise,

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:21.360
a muscle of fighting your biases.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:22.400
It's not usually coming.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.970
I believe people usually come
from a pretty good place.

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:27.580
I don't think most people are
trying to actually make anyone

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:30.650
feel othered or make them
feel like they don't belong.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.950
They don't understand that
they're questions that often come

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:35.400
out of curiosity or ignorance,

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:38.930
ends up actually having
that impact on people.

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:42.570
And so I realized that I needed
to talk about that first.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:44.950
And once you actually work on that muscle

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:47.690
in your own community, you
actually start to understand

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:49.890
what it feels like to be
more cognizant of that

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:50.723
in the rest of the world.

00:23:50.723 --> 00:23:53.280
So I saw it as something
that was really important

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:55.310
to start with the Jewish community first,

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:58.230
and that sermon had an
impact that I've never had

00:23:58.230 --> 00:23:59.350
any other sermon have.

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:01.820
It basically kind of
went viral for a sermon

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:02.863
in the Jewish world.

00:24:04.867 --> 00:24:07.620
And I think that it was
hitting at a particular time,

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:10.110
again, timing is everything,
where the community was willing

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:13.930
to think about it and listen,
and it's really shaped

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:16.830
the conversation for our
community, kind of moving forward,

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.510
how we're prioritizing
some of these questions.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:22.340
And I think a sensitivity to
the way that we're responding

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:24.470
and talking to each other
in our own community.

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:27.470
So, I now have a Jews of
color group at my synagogue,

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:29.520
which is not a group that existed before.

00:24:30.385 --> 00:24:33.080
And I think that the ideas we
don't wanna remain separate,

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:34.750
but we also wanna have
a safe space to talk

00:24:34.750 --> 00:24:38.113
about some of those things
that are still challenging.

00:24:40.446 --> 00:24:43.310
And that group, for example,
wanted to have a meeting

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:46.080
and talk post Buffalo about
what it is to be both a person

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:47.950
who might be Black and Jewish.

00:24:47.950 --> 00:24:50.980
And there's sort of some of
the hatred that was coming out

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:55.980
of the gunman's mouth was both
this replacement theory idea

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:59.600
of that both is rooted in
deeply antisemitic views,

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:04.600
as well as racist views against Blacks.

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:09.610
So I think that it's been really
a very interesting journey

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.370
to think about what this means.

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:15.660
I would say that I credit my community

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:20.110
absolutely for embracing
me and I think ultimately

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:23.590
the people in our community
with all of its diversity,

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:25.620
I mean, ironically, the
one thing I would say

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:28.801
is I think that when I wanted
to become the senior rabbi,

00:25:28.801 --> 00:25:30.310
I'd already been in my congregation

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.310
for eight years as the senior Cantor

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:34.550
and so I was known in the community.

00:25:34.550 --> 00:25:37.740
And I don't think that it
was an issue at all anymore

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:39.940
that I was Asian, but
it was very interesting

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:41.940
that it wasn't issue that I was a woman,

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:47.314
because that was the gap for people.

00:25:47.314 --> 00:25:50.010
At the time there was not another woman

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:52.480
leading a large synagogue
in all of New York.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:56.830
Like there still is not
a woman in Manhattan

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.517
leading a synagogue,
that's a large synagogue

00:25:59.517 --> 00:26:01.230
and we have a dozen of them.

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:04.340
So I think that there's
still, it's 50 years,

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:05.790
which as I said, feels like a long time,

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:07.010
but it's also not that long

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:08.710
and we just haven't
quite gotten there yet.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:11.060
And so for them, the bigger leap

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.750
was imagining a woman in charge.

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:15.610
They had become comfortable with the woman

00:26:15.610 --> 00:26:18.790
in a number two position, but
I think it's very different.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:21.510
And it came out in
different ways that I felt

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:23.810
when I was applying for that job,

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:26.690
eventually people selected me.

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:29.280
So I think that obviously it wasn't enough

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:30.990
for people to not kind of say,

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:31.960
we're ready to take this leap,

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:36.370
but it's interesting how my
president, who at the time,

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.030
said that as soon as it was announced,

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:40.750
we actually happened to go
to a large convention of Jews

00:26:40.750 --> 00:26:43.470
called The Biennial
Gathering a week later.

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:46.607
He said he could not believe
how many people said to him,

00:26:46.607 --> 00:26:49.050
"What a brave decision you made."

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:51.507
And he thought, "Wow, they're
really characterizing this

00:26:51.507 --> 00:26:55.927
"as like, it's brave to
name a Korean female rabbi

00:26:55.927 --> 00:26:57.940
"as the head of Central Synagogue."

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:00.250
And so that just gives you
a sense of where that was,

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:02.450
that was nine years ago.

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:05.520
- Yeah, wow, such a helpful perspective,

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:06.450
all the things you've shared

00:27:06.450 --> 00:27:09.850
and especially that
last component as well.

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:11.090
But just to go back a little bit,

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:13.770
I mean, I'm impressed and encouraged

00:27:13.770 --> 00:27:17.550
one, at your willingness to
lean in, in your own house

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:19.620
and for the ways that
people seem to go along

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:22.890
on that journey with you and
really have that introspection,

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:24.920
'cause even as you were
talking, I was imagining

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:26.767
just ways that people could
react in that situation.

00:27:26.767 --> 00:27:28.570
I mean just based on all
the things we've seen,

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:32.300
so many different ways of
denial or 'cause like you said,

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:35.410
even if it's not from a
place of intentional hurt,

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:37.100
that can sometimes put
up a wall and the barrier

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:39.680
where people don't really want
to acknowledge what's there.

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:41.420
So as much as people were willing to lean

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:44.610
into that conversation and
to you to be able to listen

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:48.107
to what people were
bringing to you as well.

00:27:48.107 --> 00:27:50.020
But to your last point,
I think that just speaks

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:52.650
of where things are, and
again, this is me speculating,

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:55.320
but you being in this role as a woman

00:27:55.320 --> 00:27:58.130
and people trusting you and
entering into that space

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.750
with you and walking
through these hard topics,

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:04.300
because obviously they
are difficult challenges

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:05.420
for any of us to walk through,

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:07.970
but it seems like there's
more and more layers there

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:10.050
as they're looking to you as a leader,

00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:12.370
but also having to reconcile the fact

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:16.350
that you are one of the few,
especially in your area,

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:19.280
and how that has also
likely had an impact.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:20.690
I mean, even that comment
about people saying

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:23.350
that was the bravest
decision, it's very reflective

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:26.210
in a lot of ways about
people's perceptions.

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:30.130
And not only like they were
saying that for the president

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:32.330
and whoever was involved
to make that decision,

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:34.680
but it seems like that would
also have an impact on people

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:36.820
being willing to follow in a sense,

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:37.860
not that they're following you,

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:40.130
they're ultimately following
God in the higher power,

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:42.390
but you, in that role as a facilitator.

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:45.580
So again, I'm just kind of
reflecting as you're sharing

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:47.020
all those pieces.

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:48.890
But one other question
that did come to mind

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:51.820
is just, as you mentioned
that there aren't other women

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:55.290
of large synagogues who
are in this same type

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:58.910
of leadership role, what has
that process been like for you?

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.160
I mean, is there enough, not
that you don't have a cohort

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:05.600
among your peers, but is that
I won't call it attention,

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.590
but I'm just curious, I
guess how that experience

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:08.423
has been for you.

00:29:08.423 --> 00:29:10.393
I don't want to pre-label
it or presuppose,

00:29:11.399 --> 00:29:13.680
just to kind of give you room
to comment on that as well.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:15.330
- I guess the good news I would say

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:18.000
is that it really has changed, even since,

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:19.540
I've been in this role
for eight years now,

00:29:19.540 --> 00:29:23.940
so since I was named nine
years ago, it has changed.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:27.600
There's a woman was named
to a large congregation

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:32.600
in Brooklyn and then
soon after up in Toronto.

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:35.970
And so, not yet in Manhattan as many,

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:37.890
although I certainly
have female colleague,

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:41.050
but of like the large communities
that you started to see it

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:43.370
changing in the last
especially five years.

00:29:43.370 --> 00:29:45.600
And so that's also been encouraging

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.740
and I'm part of a larger rabbi cohort

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:50.930
that studies together
in Jerusalem every year.

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:54.980
And that now has like a real cadre

00:29:54.980 --> 00:29:59.780
of like strong women rabbis,
so that's been wonderful.

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:02.720
But I still, when I sit at tables

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:04.623
and especially interfaith tables,

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:08.370
I often am still like the
only woman in the room.

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:12.520
I'm part of a religious
coalition that is organized

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:15.350
by the Cardinal Dolan here in New York

00:30:15.350 --> 00:30:18.350
and there literally were
no women at that table.

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:20.527
And I actually opened
my big mouth and said,

00:30:20.527 --> 00:30:22.947
"I notice that this is a
coalition of religious leaders,

00:30:22.947 --> 00:30:25.307
"you don't have a single
woman sitting at this table

00:30:25.307 --> 00:30:26.760
"of your executive group."

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:28.610
So you open your big mouth,
then you get invited,

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:29.830
so I have to show up.

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:31.040
Now there are two of us,

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:36.040
but the fact is that in
many faith traditions,

00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:38.270
they still do not have women in positions

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:41.060
of religious leadership with
specific kinds of titles.

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:45.635
And so it's not just about
my own faith tradition,

00:30:45.635 --> 00:30:48.060
it's in the larger faith world and space.

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.890
There's still real barriers for women,

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:52.660
the stain glass ceiling, we call it.

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:56.660
And I think there's
still a lot of work here

00:30:56.660 --> 00:31:00.290
and the implications are
not just that you're losing

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:04.580
50% of the potential
leadership that you could have,

00:31:04.580 --> 00:31:08.000
I actually think it's even
more dramatic than that

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:09.260
in terms of what is lost.

00:31:09.260 --> 00:31:12.410
I think that people's theology is shaped

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:16.140
by the people who convey the message.

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:19.518
And it's just interesting, I mean, people,

00:31:19.518 --> 00:31:23.530
I used to grow up and say
things like, oh, Rabbi Stern,

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:26.220
he was a rabbi of a community
that I used to lead.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:28.210
His voice was the voice of God,

00:31:28.210 --> 00:31:30.210
that's what I imagined
God's voice to sound like.

00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:31.660
It was Jack Stern, and believe me,

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:33.140
he was a brilliant preacher,

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:35.550
I could imagine God
sounding like Jack Stern.

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:39.530
But that kind of refrain
of like the voice of God

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:42.200
being their rabbi, just
as the transmitter,

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.510
think about what that does
for your conception of it.

00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:47.550
Now what's interesting is now
that I'm the senior rabbi,

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:49.480
I don't actually think that people think

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:52.130
that God sounds like me as much as now.

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:54.300
I think actually they understand God

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.160
in a totally different way, a
little less like from on high,

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:58.420
I think it's partly generational,

00:31:58.420 --> 00:32:00.920
I think it's partly the
way women are perceived

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:03.000
and I don't wanna make
huge generalizations,

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:05.360
but I think that it's
shifted our theology.

00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:07.050
It's shifted our religious language,

00:32:07.050 --> 00:32:08.930
it's shifted the way we create community.

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:11.450
It's so much deeper
than just whether or not

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:13.160
we can put women in positions.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:15.040
When you're talking about
spiritual leadership,

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:17.840
it actually impacts the
entire way you actually think

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:19.550
about the life of the spirit.

00:32:19.550 --> 00:32:23.240
So I think that it's profound,
the change that's happened

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:24.750
in 50 years since women have entered

00:32:24.750 --> 00:32:26.319
the rabbinate in our community.

00:32:26.319 --> 00:32:28.050
- Yeah, that's really wonderful to hear.

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:29.920
And it seems like it's just
like, as you mentioned,

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:32.280
just perspective in a lot of ways too.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:34.400
And so many aspects of, I mean,

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:36.780
when we think about the reflection
of God in the community,

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:40.110
it's not one or the
other, it's all people.

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:42.170
So to hear you to speak about those things

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:43.530
in just the practical
ways you've seen that

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.290
is just also encouraging as well.

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:50.200
And I would imagine this
having just generational,

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:52.010
or will have generational impacts as well.

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:53.650
I guess some of that remains to be seen,

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:56.507
but I'm just thinking
forward in that realm as well

00:32:56.507 --> 00:32:57.930
and so definitely appreciate the way

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:00.210
that you've brought so many
of those things to light

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:02.200
and then all the work that
you're continuing to do.

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:04.770
'Cause I imagine it's not light work

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.065
even as we have already
been talking about.

00:33:07.065 --> 00:33:11.320
And on that, I mean, just
balance is a word that I use

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:14.240
cautiously at this point
in time, but I'm wondering

00:33:14.240 --> 00:33:16.460
just to kind of move the
conversation a little bit

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:18.000
outside of your specific role,

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:21.920
but also think about how you
attempt to balance or juggle

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:25.080
or manage as it were all
the many different roles

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:26.610
that you have and responsibilities,

00:33:26.610 --> 00:33:28.600
even in the midst of the
things that continue to happen,

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:30.440
because you talked about
some of the communities

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:33.460
that you've been involved
in, in your own congregation,

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:35.220
the groups that have started
up, even the conversation

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:37.410
you had the last few days
about the situation in Buffalo.

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:40.090
So how do you manage your
time from that standpoint

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:43.630
to still be able to invest
and serve in your congregation

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:45.920
in the community, but then
also to be able to have people

00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:47.123
pour into you as well.

00:33:50.091 --> 00:33:52.190
- Balance, I think what
does that word mean?

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:53.890
I think the image that comes to mind

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.120
is like being on one of
those like little seat

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:59.690
of like those Teeter-totters,
where like you put your leg

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:02.220
on each side and like,
the idea that you get

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:04.220
to a point of balance, maybe
you're like, "I got it."

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:06.110
And then you're immediately back and forth

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:09.260
and like the entire time all
you're doing is adjusting.

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:11.560
I think that's kind of what
I think my balance has been,

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:13.420
like, it's just constantly adjusting.

00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:14.897
Every once in a while, I can
have a moment where like,

00:34:14.897 --> 00:34:18.040
"Ah, I'm in balance" and then it's back.

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:21.600
So, I think, I don't know if
that's what I'm going for,

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:24.490
I think I wanna be able to feel
like at the end of the day,

00:34:24.490 --> 00:34:27.480
the things that I have paid attention to,

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.660
which is ultimately gonna be
what my life is made up of,

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:31.493
that those are the things

00:34:31.493 --> 00:34:34.020
that I wanted to have paid attention to.

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:38.630
And I've gotten better about
calendaring that into my life

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:41.623
so that like, I actually
make sure that I calendar in,

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:47.132
my daughter's like basketball game,

00:34:47.132 --> 00:34:48.830
and then if that's in there

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:52.770
that I'm not just programming
around or scheduling over it,

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:56.580
that I actually, make sure I
have time for my meditation.

00:34:56.580 --> 00:34:59.150
That is part of my own personal practice

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:01.800
or going and exercising a little bit

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:04.210
so that I can have like
mental and physical health.

00:35:04.210 --> 00:35:06.380
So I think that what
I've gotten better about

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:09.290
is scheduling that and working my life

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:11.330
to make sure that those elements in.

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:12.870
Sometimes it has to get canceled,

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:16.113
sometimes it has to get moved,
but it's that constant work.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:20.130
One of the things I have
come to understand better

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:21.840
and it probably took
till about 10 years ago

00:35:21.840 --> 00:35:24.850
when I finally started doing
more spiritual practice work

00:35:24.850 --> 00:35:29.080
and meditation is I
used to think of things

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:31.780
like taking care of myself.

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:33.640
Self-care has like all
sorts of connotations.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:35.330
It can sometimes be problematic,

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:39.230
but I used to think of it
as in way sort of indulgent.

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:42.970
And I went on my first sort
of spiritual practice retreat

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:44.700
with the Institute for
Jewish Spirituality,

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:47.750
I guess it's already like 10 years ago,

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:51.410
five days, mostly silent
meditation retreat

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:53.660
and with some learning and some yoga

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:55.270
and a lot of meditation.

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:57.540
And it was like a revelation for me.

00:35:57.540 --> 00:35:59.742
It sort of helped me understand

00:35:59.742 --> 00:36:03.255
that when I am actually not
feeling in some ways whole,

00:36:03.255 --> 00:36:06.210
I've got nothing left to give in some way.

00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:08.750
And so I started to understand
that caring for myself

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:12.548
is a form of caring for
God, caring for others.

00:36:12.548 --> 00:36:17.470
And so I don't feel as
apologetic for making that time

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:19.470
that I think I used to in my younger days,

00:36:19.470 --> 00:36:21.963
feel much more that way.

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:24.530
It's still hard to find finally,

00:36:24.530 --> 00:36:29.410
and I think that's continued work.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:31.370
- Yeah, well, I appreciate
you sharing that as well

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:33.960
'cause I think so many
people have started to pay

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:36.190
a bunch more attention to
that, and it's just refreshing

00:36:36.190 --> 00:36:37.990
and encouraging to hear you stating that

00:36:37.990 --> 00:36:39.640
as a leader as well,

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:41.480
just knowing how important that time is.

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:44.430
I mean, one humorous example
I always share with people

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:46.630
is just the lack of a
commute for me personally

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:48.880
and not realizing how much
of that I use for processing

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:51.260
when that commute turned
into the one minute commute

00:36:51.260 --> 00:36:53.940
from the attic makeshift
office, back to home life

00:36:53.940 --> 00:36:55.663
and homework and all the other things

00:36:55.663 --> 00:36:57.150
that come with that realizing,

00:36:57.150 --> 00:36:59.010
just feeling like things
were more frazzled.

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:01.350
And so no, oh, that
wasn't having that time

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:03.020
was actually helpful and I didn't realize

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:05.750
how much I was using
it to process and reset

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:09.220
and just have balance
in that sense as well.

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:11.210
So you hear you talk about those
retreats that you've taken,

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:13.630
how important that is, and
then just even thinking

00:37:13.630 --> 00:37:15.200
from a biblical reference standpoint

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:18.930
to if our bodies are temples
and are we actually taking care

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:22.270
of our temples or just output,
output, output all the time

00:37:22.270 --> 00:37:23.470
with no care for that.

00:37:23.470 --> 00:37:26.410
So I appreciate you walking
us through that as well

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:27.760
and it's the importance of scheduling,

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:30.920
sometimes it makes it
feel, I guess, less, well,

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:32.580
it is less spontaneous
and less meaningful,

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:33.930
but I think at the end of
the day, like you mentioned,

00:37:33.930 --> 00:37:36.100
it just important to have those pieces

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:37.283
and to know that there is meaning

00:37:37.283 --> 00:37:39.130
that comes from that
intentional scheduling

00:37:39.130 --> 00:37:40.760
of that basketball game for your daughter

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:42.660
and how important that is for both of you.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:44.770
So, definitely appreciate you taking--

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:46.700
- Well, I mean, and I think this wisdom

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:49.350
of just the importance
of some of that time,

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:52.170
I mean, that is a foundational
principle of Judaism

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:56.420
that we are actually not
meant to work day in, day out

00:37:56.420 --> 00:37:57.840
and create every day of the week.

00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:00.200
We actually have to take rest

00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:04.850
and Shabbat is actually
not just utilitarian

00:38:04.850 --> 00:38:06.910
so that we can rest up so
we can go back and work,

00:38:06.910 --> 00:38:09.220
it's actually the point of it all.

00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:12.510
And I would say that
maybe the most profound

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:13.910
spiritual practice of my life

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:17.820
is that I really do make
Shabbat for myself and my family

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:19.076
and that has sustained us.

00:38:19.076 --> 00:38:23.480
Even though in my field of
work, I work on Friday nights

00:38:23.480 --> 00:38:25.890
and lead services, to me
though leading services

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:29.030
is when I'm in the flow
and I find tremendous joy

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:32.433
and that does not feel like
the work of my job at all.

00:38:34.242 --> 00:38:37.730
And this Saturday morning,
I often am leading services,

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:39.940
although I'm also off some,
because I have a big team

00:38:39.940 --> 00:38:42.140
and we share the responsibilities.

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:46.690
So, but Saturday afternoon
we are almost always

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:49.610
having a long leisurely unencumbered,

00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:51.810
Shabbat lunch with other families,

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:53.660
with our whole family with no electronics.

00:38:53.660 --> 00:38:58.660
And it is luxuriousness of space and time

00:38:59.030 --> 00:38:59.880
and nothing scheduled.

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:02.800
And I think there is
something about the boundaries

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:05.440
of like, I can't do
anything else, but just be,

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:07.270
that actually creates tremendous freedom.

00:39:07.270 --> 00:39:09.820
And there's such wisdom,

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:12.130
I think if I could encourage everyone

00:39:12.130 --> 00:39:14.650
and I don't mean just
people who are Jewish,

00:39:14.650 --> 00:39:18.655
but all people to truly
feel bound to take one day

00:39:18.655 --> 00:39:21.670
without creating or doing
or feeling ambitious,

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:24.900
but just to feel like this moment,

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:27.240
I don't have to do anything,
but just be with the people

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:32.240
I love, exist, enjoy, take
joy is life changing really.

00:39:33.853 --> 00:39:37.194
- Yeah, I 100% agree with you
and appreciate that reminder

00:39:37.194 --> 00:39:39.767
for all of us and the importance of it.

00:39:39.767 --> 00:39:42.820
And again, just impressed with
how, 'cause it is discipline

00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:44.061
the way that you're talking about as well,

00:39:44.061 --> 00:39:44.899
making that disciplined.
- Yeah, it actually

00:39:44.899 --> 00:39:46.210
takes discipline.

00:39:46.210 --> 00:39:48.790
In some ways, I didn't
grow up in a tradition

00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:51.750
where I felt that that was
my religious obligation,

00:39:51.750 --> 00:39:55.100
I grew up in a less
kind of observant home.

00:39:55.100 --> 00:39:56.520
And I actually, there's a part of me

00:39:56.520 --> 00:40:00.160
that was very jealous of
people who felt like commanded

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:04.020
by God in that sort of
traditional way to like not work.

00:40:04.020 --> 00:40:06.990
And it took me a while to get
to the place of recognizing

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:11.150
that that commandedness was
real and can come internally

00:40:11.150 --> 00:40:15.147
and you can decide to take
your life in that way.

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:20.460
So many of us feel like we don't
have control over our lives

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:25.060
that way, I think we have
to push back on that.

00:40:25.060 --> 00:40:26.746
- Yeah, yeah, 100% agree.

00:40:26.746 --> 00:40:30.168
And just the life and the
freedom that comes from that.

00:40:30.168 --> 00:40:33.420
Even from neuroscience
plus the expectation,

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:36.160
we can look forward to
that and have that control

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:39.320
and just all the ways that,
I mean so many aspects

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:41.350
of what you talked about
just even being in community

00:40:41.350 --> 00:40:43.490
with one another in that
space and in that time,

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:47.420
without all the outside
pressures and just how beneficial

00:40:47.420 --> 00:40:52.070
that is for us as human
beings, as God's creation

00:40:52.070 --> 00:40:53.840
and just what it does to be in community.

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:55.550
I mean, I'm trying not
to pull in all the things

00:40:55.550 --> 00:40:58.160
from our previous episodes
with oxytocin and community,

00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:00.440
but all of that is there as well.

00:41:00.440 --> 00:41:03.100
I'll let people listen
to previous episodes

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:04.280
to really dig deep into that,

00:41:04.280 --> 00:41:06.880
but it's so refreshing in some ways.

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:09.220
Even in these conversations
that we have to see

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:11.660
so many of these things come
from different components,

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:13.350
from what we know from
our faith traditions,

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:16.030
from what we know and are
learning from the neuroscience

00:41:16.030 --> 00:41:17.590
or psychology and to
know that these things

00:41:17.590 --> 00:41:19.410
are for our benefit, and there's a reason

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:21.904
that we should strive to
keep those disciplines.

00:41:21.904 --> 00:41:26.904
- I love that, I kind of geek
out on finding out the science

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:29.310
that supports the thing
that religious tradition

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:31.240
has been telling us to do all along.

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:35.100
I love that, it just kind of
confirms the wonder of this,

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:37.430
but I think it's part of it
is that you have to imagine

00:41:37.430 --> 00:41:38.990
a religious tradition that has evolved

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:40.763
over thousands of years.

00:41:41.970 --> 00:41:45.720
And I think it really truly does exist.

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:49.210
Its purpose is to kind of
help shape people's lives

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:52.820
as lives of meaning and value and purpose

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:55.900
and also help reinforce community,

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:57.240
which is so deeply important.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.970
I think in a time when
people feel more isolated

00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:03.460
than they've ever felt, more lonely,

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:05.640
lacking meaning and direction,

00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:08.180
I think that religion is the antidote.

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:11.000
I mean, I think spirituality for me,

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:14.160
it's not just about believing in God.

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:16.130
In fact, it doesn't have
to be that language,

00:42:16.130 --> 00:42:18.030
which I recognize has a
lot of baggage for people.

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:22.030
I think maybe one of the best explanations

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:24.600
I've heard and I don't
know where I heard this,

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:28.270
so I'm sorry I can't quote
it, but I'll share it,

00:42:28.270 --> 00:42:32.000
is that the opposite of
spirituality is not atheism

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:35.620
or ignorcissism, the
opposite is narcissism.

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:38.230
So if a spiritual life is
really, truly to help you

00:42:38.230 --> 00:42:42.490
get out of your own head, to
help you actually understand

00:42:42.490 --> 00:42:44.850
you are part of something
much bigger than yourself,

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:46.400
you're part of a bigger story.

00:42:46.400 --> 00:42:50.300
You are obligated to
something beyond yourself,

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:52.680
that to me is the entire purpose

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:54.670
of what a spiritual life should be.

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:57.840
And I think every faith
tradition offers structure,

00:42:57.840 --> 00:42:59.653
ritual teachings for that.

00:43:00.550 --> 00:43:03.410
I think every religious tradition
can be corrupted as well

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.160
and can be taken to extremes.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:08.330
But I think every faith
tradition offers the ability

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:10.559
for each human being to
really find their purpose

00:43:10.559 --> 00:43:14.550
and understand themselves
out of the smallness

00:43:14.550 --> 00:43:17.340
of their individual
life and in the context

00:43:17.340 --> 00:43:19.774
of something eternal and much bigger.

00:43:19.774 --> 00:43:24.190
- Yeah, so well said, yeah, 100% agree.

00:43:24.190 --> 00:43:25.720
And it's just again, so
refreshing and beautiful

00:43:25.720 --> 00:43:27.370
to hear you speaking about that as well

00:43:27.370 --> 00:43:29.990
and all the ways that we
really need to continue

00:43:29.990 --> 00:43:31.990
to move that conversation forward.

00:43:31.990 --> 00:43:33.600
And as you mentioned, especially some part

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:36.470
of something bigger and
part of a larger community,

00:43:36.470 --> 00:43:38.540
you know how important
that is for so many of us,

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:41.140
especially, even as we
let off this conversation,

00:43:41.140 --> 00:43:43.730
just saying that we have seen
aspects of ways communities

00:43:43.730 --> 00:43:45.890
have come together in the
midst of the challenges

00:43:45.890 --> 00:43:48.570
that we are facing and ways
that it was more difficult

00:43:48.570 --> 00:43:51.300
for us to do that when
we were in isolation.

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:53.890
So again, I'm hoping that
we hold onto those aspects

00:43:53.890 --> 00:43:58.890
and we don't forget those
things, as we are back in society

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:00.970
in a sense or emerging or reemerging

00:44:00.970 --> 00:44:02.940
or whatever state people are in,

00:44:02.940 --> 00:44:05.230
because there's a whole
range of different states

00:44:05.230 --> 00:44:06.240
that people are walking through that.

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:07.540
But I think is such an important thing

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:10.790
for us to be able to hold
onto in so many ways.

00:44:10.790 --> 00:44:12.310
And also this makes me think of unity,

00:44:12.310 --> 00:44:13.440
the importance of unity.

00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:16.220
I mean, especially, I
know I keep harping back

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:19.490
to what happened in the
past week, but it's been,

00:44:19.490 --> 00:44:21.340
I think an opportunity
for us to have unity,

00:44:21.340 --> 00:44:23.210
I've even seen some
things on social media,

00:44:23.210 --> 00:44:25.010
just talking about well opportunities

00:44:25.010 --> 00:44:27.010
for those in Jewish communities and Black

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:28.300
and brown communities
and other communities

00:44:28.300 --> 00:44:32.070
to come together around some
of these unfortunately common,

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:34.640
tragic situations that many
of us are walking through,

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:36.650
especially even in Asian American

00:44:36.650 --> 00:44:38.493
and in Pacific Islander
communities and the diversity

00:44:38.493 --> 00:44:41.030
that that community has,
but what are the ways

00:44:41.030 --> 00:44:43.310
that we can kind of move together in union

00:44:43.310 --> 00:44:46.290
to make sure that these
things are addressed head on,

00:44:46.290 --> 00:44:48.510
even as you've talked about
in your own congregation.

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.010
So I know that's different
than what we've been saying,

00:44:51.010 --> 00:44:52.529
but that still comes to mind when I think

00:44:52.529 --> 00:44:53.689
about these aspects of community.

00:44:53.689 --> 00:44:55.680
And I think about these
aspects of having discipline

00:44:55.680 --> 00:44:58.600
to sit down and to pause
and to rest and refresh

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:01.490
and just be in the
presence of one another.

00:45:01.490 --> 00:45:04.480
So again, it makes me think
that this is just full circle

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:05.403
in a lot of ways.

00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:07.900
At the same time, even as you're talking

00:45:07.900 --> 00:45:08.733
about some of these disciplines,

00:45:08.733 --> 00:45:11.310
and I know that there are
often things that do come up,

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:13.650
I mean, situations come up that throw us

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:16.986
from that disciplined arena.

00:45:16.986 --> 00:45:21.350
We have to have margins for
flexibility and adjustments

00:45:21.350 --> 00:45:24.340
for impromptu conversations
and those types of things.

00:45:24.340 --> 00:45:26.370
So it's gonna have you
share about how that happens

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:29.330
in your life as well, but
also just want to highlight

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:31.760
for those who don't know,
a very specific situation

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:34.800
where that happened for
you back in January,

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:37.180
when you received a call from a gunman

00:45:37.180 --> 00:45:42.180
who had congregants in a
hostage situation in Texas,

00:45:42.310 --> 00:45:44.600
and how you responded to that situation

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:46.660
in a very quick thinking manner.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:48.210
So I wonder if you just as much as going,

00:45:48.210 --> 00:45:51.040
kind of take us through
that and what you learned

00:45:51.040 --> 00:45:53.030
in that moment, 'cause
again, obviously something

00:45:53.030 --> 00:45:55.200
that wasn't planned that didn't fit

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:57.690
into your normal routine, but
that you had to respond to.

00:45:57.690 --> 00:45:59.500
And also I would imagine to decide

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:01.613
how urgent is this in actuality?

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:08.270
- Well, I received a
voicemail on Shabbat afternoon

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:12.250
after services from a
rabbi who let me know

00:46:12.250 --> 00:46:17.090
that he was being held
hostage with three others

00:46:17.090 --> 00:46:20.470
by a gunman who had bombs and had a gun

00:46:20.470 --> 00:46:22.100
and was threatening their lives.

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:25.120
And he asked for me to call them back.

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:29.960
It took me a few moments
to even figure out

00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:33.500
if this was real, did
a quick Google search,

00:46:33.500 --> 00:46:35.670
tried to see if there was
anything in the news yet,

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:38.030
which there wasn't at that point.

00:46:38.030 --> 00:46:39.850
But realized very quickly

00:46:39.850 --> 00:46:43.030
I needed to actually
call this person back.

00:46:43.030 --> 00:46:45.010
The rabbi picked up the phone

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:47.480
and then quickly passed
the phone to the gunman

00:46:47.480 --> 00:46:52.480
who I realized very quickly,
this was not a crank call,

00:46:53.033 --> 00:46:56.310
that this was real and terrifying.

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:04.440
And essentially the gunman took hold

00:47:04.790 --> 00:47:08.720
of a really age old anti-Semitic trope

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:13.720
that Jews kind of basically
control government

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:18.970
and control others and
have outsized influence

00:47:18.970 --> 00:47:21.280
the sort of, kind of
conspiracy theory model

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:23.400
of Jewish power.

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:28.400
And he called me, he had
done some internet research

00:47:28.460 --> 00:47:30.610
maybe because I lead Central Synagogue,

00:47:30.610 --> 00:47:32.930
and Central sounds like
it's sort of the center,

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:36.530
he's from England and in
England they have a chief rabbi.

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:38.580
I think he was looking for the equivalent

00:47:38.580 --> 00:47:42.530
and he saw a picture of me
when I went to the White House

00:47:42.530 --> 00:47:43.940
pictured with Obama.

00:47:43.940 --> 00:47:48.920
So he was like, you know the
president, you know Obama.

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:51.120
Honestly, I'm not really
sure why he picked me,

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:56.120
but he believed, he believed
that I could free a person

00:47:56.390 --> 00:47:58.980
he called his sister, although
she wasn't his sister,

00:47:58.980 --> 00:48:03.980
but a terrorist who had been
convicted and was sitting

00:48:03.990 --> 00:48:07.250
in a federal prison 20 minutes
from that synagogue in Texas.

00:48:07.250 --> 00:48:11.710
And so he basically gave
me an hour to free her

00:48:11.710 --> 00:48:13.600
and bring her to the synagogue.

00:48:13.600 --> 00:48:16.667
And he said, "If you do that,
then everyone can stay safe

00:48:16.667 --> 00:48:18.557
"and if you don't, well, I have a bomb

00:48:18.557 --> 00:48:21.077
"strapped in my backpack and
I've got bombs in New York

00:48:21.077 --> 00:48:22.787
"and I have bombs in Brooklyn."

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:27.070
Wasn't sure about the bombs
in New York and Brooklyn,

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:30.370
but I was quite certain
that this man had a gun

00:48:30.370 --> 00:48:32.780
and probably a bomb with him

00:48:32.780 --> 00:48:35.890
and that he was prepared to
die, that was very clear.

00:48:35.890 --> 00:48:38.767
He said that on the phone several times,

00:48:38.767 --> 00:48:41.460
"I love death more than you love life."

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:46.460
And I mean, it was a surreal
terrifying responsibility.

00:48:51.570 --> 00:48:55.990
I felt completely responsible
for these four people's lives

00:48:55.990 --> 00:48:59.320
even as I knew in my mind

00:48:59.320 --> 00:49:02.738
that I was mostly
powerless to do anything.

00:49:02.738 --> 00:49:07.573
The little that I could
control was buying some time,

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:11.870
hoping to keep him calm, making assurances

00:49:11.870 --> 00:49:14.010
until someone could come and save them,

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:18.030
which I thought could be quick,
because I had already heard

00:49:18.030 --> 00:49:20.168
that police were on the scene there.

00:49:20.168 --> 00:49:23.990
As the situation
unfolded, it took 11 hours

00:49:23.990 --> 00:49:28.130
before they actually freed themselves

00:49:28.130 --> 00:49:30.820
with the rabbi throwing
a chair at the gunman

00:49:30.820 --> 00:49:34.796
and running out the door
and the FBI coming in

00:49:34.796 --> 00:49:37.510
and ultimately killing the gunman.

00:49:37.510 --> 00:49:41.950
So it was a really unreal situation.

00:49:41.950 --> 00:49:46.950
I think that it made, I think
every Jew around the world

00:49:48.590 --> 00:49:51.290
felt that they were held hostage

00:49:51.290 --> 00:49:52.730
for those 11 hours with them.

00:49:52.730 --> 00:49:55.660
I mean, we were watching
in a wait from Israel,

00:49:55.660 --> 00:49:59.620
the France to everywhere in
America, I think it was...

00:49:59.620 --> 00:50:00.870
And I think that many Americans,

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:04.223
you didn't have to be Jewish
to feel the terror of that,

00:50:05.130 --> 00:50:08.730
the horror of his demands.

00:50:08.730 --> 00:50:12.120
And I think it's pretty clear

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:13.950
there's a man who suffered
from some mental illness,

00:50:13.950 --> 00:50:18.360
which his family concurred,
but he was spouting out

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:20.590
like antisemitic trip,
which he had learned,

00:50:20.590 --> 00:50:24.810
and which is too much,
not just in the air,

00:50:24.810 --> 00:50:26.955
but too much in the news,

00:50:26.955 --> 00:50:29.280
it's been mainstreamed these ideas.

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:32.220
And that was part of
what was so frightening.

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:36.510
I think I felt personally very
vulnerable in those moments.

00:50:36.510 --> 00:50:37.970
Certainly not as much as the four

00:50:37.970 --> 00:50:39.040
that were being held hostage,

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:43.170
but, and I think that we're
still processing what it means,

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:45.465
I think, to live in a
world, which is like that.

00:50:45.465 --> 00:50:50.465
And some of that same
kind of antisemitism,

00:50:51.030 --> 00:50:54.580
which fuels white supremacy
is some of the stuff

00:50:54.580 --> 00:50:58.040
that is being spouted by
the gunman from Buffalo,

00:50:58.040 --> 00:50:59.980
that like, I think for us to think

00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:04.980
that that kind of rhetoric
doesn't turn violent,

00:51:05.065 --> 00:51:06.800
is to not look at history

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:08.110
and to not be honest with ourselves.

00:51:08.110 --> 00:51:09.820
So this has to be the kind of thing

00:51:09.820 --> 00:51:14.820
that is not just, it has to be wiped out

00:51:17.420 --> 00:51:18.750
immediately upon hearing it.

00:51:18.750 --> 00:51:20.940
And I think there's been
way too much acceptance

00:51:20.940 --> 00:51:22.603
of this kind of rhetoric
and under the guise

00:51:22.603 --> 00:51:24.844
of sort of free speech.

00:51:24.844 --> 00:51:26.053
I think it's very threatening.

00:51:26.053 --> 00:51:28.820
- Yeah, well, first of
all, I just appreciate you

00:51:28.820 --> 00:51:30.150
taking us through that as well.

00:51:30.150 --> 00:51:32.020
Obviously that was a difficult situation

00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:34.010
while it was happening, I
imagine not necessarily easy

00:51:34.010 --> 00:51:35.190
to kind of walk through it again,

00:51:35.190 --> 00:51:38.570
but just want to acknowledge
that first of all,

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:41.430
but then also just to
thank you for just sharing

00:51:41.430 --> 00:51:43.480
the important lessons with us as well,

00:51:43.480 --> 00:51:46.350
especially as we've
talked about the rhetoric,

00:51:46.350 --> 00:51:50.930
because again, it so has
so many practical outputs

00:51:50.930 --> 00:51:52.930
and outcomes, even in the
same way that we talked

00:51:52.930 --> 00:51:55.540
about on the other side,
just the impactfulness

00:51:55.540 --> 00:51:58.350
of the policies that allows
you to walk into the role

00:51:58.350 --> 00:52:01.770
that you are in a lot of ways
now, but on the other side,

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:04.670
the power of the rhetoric that
can do the complete opposite

00:52:04.670 --> 00:52:05.830
and lead to loss of life.

00:52:05.830 --> 00:52:08.810
So, I mean, I think it's
really important and healthy

00:52:08.810 --> 00:52:11.350
for us to kind of hold
both of those together

00:52:11.350 --> 00:52:12.950
and just to think about
ways that we can continue

00:52:12.950 --> 00:52:14.450
to move forward.

00:52:14.450 --> 00:52:17.160
So I know appreciate you being
willing to go to that level

00:52:17.160 --> 00:52:19.780
and just all that you did
in that situation as well.

00:52:19.780 --> 00:52:23.820
I mean, just the quick
thinking and having to process

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:27.053
and navigate simultaneously
is not an easy place to be.

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:30.220
- Well, I mean, but part
of what you're lifting up,

00:52:30.220 --> 00:52:32.360
which I think is a thread that
I hadn't really thought about

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:37.010
as much is just, we often think
of any of these situations,

00:52:37.010 --> 00:52:39.730
whether it's me being
kind of like, oh, look,

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:41.470
there's like the first Asian
rabbi and she's a woman.

00:52:41.470 --> 00:52:45.530
And it's sort of like,
almost like an unusual case.

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:47.955
And on the flip side,
it's sort of the negative,

00:52:47.955 --> 00:52:49.660
you take someone like this gunman,

00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:51.110
you say he's like a lone wolf,

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:54.090
he's this crazy one singular
person who does this.

00:52:54.090 --> 00:52:58.230
What we actually often ignore
are the systemic underpinnings

00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:01.010
that allow either the kind
of maybe exceptional case

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:03.610
in a good way, or the
exceptional case in a dangerous,

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:07.210
violent way that it's almost,
no one is a lone wolf.

00:53:07.210 --> 00:53:12.210
There are systems and biases and tropes

00:53:12.740 --> 00:53:16.440
and all sorts of stuff that
kind of like inform a person

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:18.020
that gets them to that place.

00:53:18.020 --> 00:53:21.590
So I think that with the Buffalo
shooter, with this gunman,

00:53:21.590 --> 00:53:23.910
he acted alone perhaps in that moment,

00:53:23.910 --> 00:53:26.890
but he did not come to his
views, which were so dangerous.

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:27.810
I mean, both of those guys

00:53:27.810 --> 00:53:30.290
were absolutely pre-meditating their acts.

00:53:30.290 --> 00:53:32.840
One drove 200 miles to the grocery store.

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:34.430
This one came all the way from England

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:37.360
and bought an airplane ticket
and worked his way down

00:53:37.360 --> 00:53:40.050
to that specific synagogue
that was the closest synagogue

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:41.650
he could find to the prison

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:44.320
that he wanted to free the prisoner from.

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:46.950
So like it was highly premeditated.

00:53:46.950 --> 00:53:48.060
So even if it was singular,

00:53:48.060 --> 00:53:50.370
there was a whole structure around him

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.090
thinking that this was what he had to do.

00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:54.500
He didn't go to the church that was close

00:53:54.500 --> 00:53:56.690
to that federal prison,
he went to the synagogue

00:53:56.690 --> 00:53:58.370
for a very specific reason.

00:53:58.370 --> 00:54:00.170
So I think that, like
those kinds of things

00:54:00.170 --> 00:54:01.003
that we have to think about,

00:54:01.003 --> 00:54:02.890
and it can happen in the positive way too,

00:54:02.890 --> 00:54:05.160
that you can have cases that come up.

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:08.160
But I know that I was
supported by a whole structure

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:12.670
of policies and mentors and communities

00:54:12.670 --> 00:54:16.100
that sort of like taught
me and helped me understand

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:18.290
what was possible and then enabled

00:54:18.290 --> 00:54:19.488
all those things to be possible.

00:54:19.488 --> 00:54:22.838
So anyway, those things matter.

00:54:22.838 --> 00:54:24.912
- Yeah, that's really well said.

00:54:24.912 --> 00:54:27.781
And in a sense it puts onus of all of us

00:54:27.781 --> 00:54:31.030
as members of our communities
to think about the influence

00:54:31.030 --> 00:54:33.590
that we have with every
single person around us.

00:54:33.590 --> 00:54:35.330
I mean, I often talk about
that if I'm giving a talk

00:54:35.330 --> 00:54:37.280
around mental health advocacy.

00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:39.640
'Cause I think we don't often
all think about ourselves

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:42.970
as influencers because that
word has been so misused.

00:54:42.970 --> 00:54:44.350
- Corrupted, yeah.

00:54:44.350 --> 00:54:46.950
- I don't know if I wanna go
there, but at the same time,

00:54:46.950 --> 00:54:48.840
I often try and challenge
people to think about,

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:51.670
well, who are the people
immediately around you

00:54:51.670 --> 00:54:54.950
and how do they react when you
talk about specific things?

00:54:54.950 --> 00:54:57.250
So obviously I'm often painting that

00:54:57.250 --> 00:54:58.120
in terms of mental health,

00:54:58.120 --> 00:54:59.340
but how do you talk about mental health?

00:54:59.340 --> 00:55:01.190
Do you talk about it pejoratively

00:55:01.190 --> 00:55:03.650
and does that rub off on those around you?

00:55:03.650 --> 00:55:04.880
Do they also talk about it that way

00:55:04.880 --> 00:55:06.190
so that when someone now runs

00:55:06.190 --> 00:55:08.160
into a mental health challenge,

00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:10.670
it's seen as completely negative
because that's the culture

00:55:10.670 --> 00:55:13.100
and the atmosphere that
we've talked about that in.

00:55:13.100 --> 00:55:15.330
And I think you're saying
that in the same way as well

00:55:15.330 --> 00:55:18.210
versus do we talk about things
in ways that are hopeful

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:22.680
or informed by truth and healing

00:55:22.680 --> 00:55:24.550
and opportunities for community,

00:55:24.550 --> 00:55:26.350
because all that will have effects.

00:55:26.350 --> 00:55:27.930
And sometimes, I mean,
that can be too late

00:55:27.930 --> 00:55:30.180
to think about all the time,

00:55:30.180 --> 00:55:32.240
but I think we're oftentimes ignore that.

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:33.730
I mean, myself, I can
think about that as well.

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:36.570
We often ignore that
and we just need to pay

00:55:36.570 --> 00:55:38.660
that much more attention to it,
to think about all the lives

00:55:38.660 --> 00:55:40.360
that we're influencing around us,

00:55:40.360 --> 00:55:42.340
because it will have
these larger implications.

00:55:42.340 --> 00:55:44.930
And I think everything that
you've shared on both sides,

00:55:44.930 --> 00:55:46.863
has really highlighted that as well.

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:54.200
So a lot for us to think about, and again,

00:55:54.200 --> 00:55:57.370
just, I guess I'm feeling
also at the same time,

00:55:57.370 --> 00:55:59.310
it's challenging 'cause I feel grateful

00:55:59.310 --> 00:56:01.020
and then also a weight of responsibility.

00:56:01.020 --> 00:56:02.837
In a sense, I'm grateful
because I feel like,

00:56:02.837 --> 00:56:04.950
and this is gonna sound
like self-promotion,

00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:07.930
but just the ways we've been
able to do that on this podcast

00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:10.400
with so many people who
have been able to share

00:56:10.400 --> 00:56:12.920
the ways that they're
having impact locally,

00:56:12.920 --> 00:56:14.350
in their families and their communities,

00:56:14.350 --> 00:56:17.540
but then also on much larger
scales and nationally as well.

00:56:17.540 --> 00:56:20.210
And just as much as we
can continue to elevate

00:56:20.210 --> 00:56:22.320
both the small and the big
things that are happening,

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:23.403
I think are just important for us.

00:56:23.403 --> 00:56:24.980
And I feel like you've been doing that

00:56:24.980 --> 00:56:26.730
in this conversation
too, just even in the way

00:56:26.730 --> 00:56:28.670
I keep going back to the
things that you mentioned

00:56:28.670 --> 00:56:30.467
about the Shabbat and just the community

00:56:30.467 --> 00:56:31.420
and the piece that's there.

00:56:31.420 --> 00:56:34.190
I mean, I almost in a sense,
and this is my, sorry,

00:56:34.190 --> 00:56:36.380
it's my addiction related
research coming out again,

00:56:36.380 --> 00:56:39.557
but I feel like I have a craving
for that type of atmosphere

00:56:39.557 --> 00:56:41.250
and that environment
and thinking about ways

00:56:41.250 --> 00:56:44.960
to be more proactive about
trying to include aspects of that

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:47.120
in my own life, because
it's easy for that to slip

00:56:47.120 --> 00:56:49.520
without that discipline
and dedication as well.

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:51.200
So again, a lot of full circle moments

00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:54.260
to them having in this
conversation as I'm just reflecting

00:56:54.260 --> 00:56:56.810
on many of the things that
you're sharing as well.

00:56:56.810 --> 00:56:59.010
I did also just wanna end
just talking a little bit

00:56:59.010 --> 00:57:01.210
about hope because we've
alluded to it a lot

00:57:01.210 --> 00:57:02.620
as we've been going
through the conversation,

00:57:02.620 --> 00:57:04.420
you've talked about things
that have been given you hope

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:07.430
even in your role and
amongst your confidence,

00:57:07.430 --> 00:57:10.120
but I'm curious just what
that word means to you

00:57:10.120 --> 00:57:12.740
on a day to day basis, in
the midst of everything

00:57:12.740 --> 00:57:14.050
that we experience and in the midst

00:57:14.050 --> 00:57:15.350
of everything that you do.

00:57:17.670 --> 00:57:22.670
- Well, I ended my sermon
the week after Colleyville,

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:28.310
sharing a blessing that
is part of our tradition.

00:57:28.310 --> 00:57:30.830
And it is a blessing that we say

00:57:30.830 --> 00:57:34.510
before we read from the
Book of Lamentations.

00:57:34.510 --> 00:57:38.232
Lamentations that maybe the
saddest book of the Bible,

00:57:38.232 --> 00:57:41.630
and it's about Jerusalem
being sacked and destroyed.

00:57:41.630 --> 00:57:44.180
It uses imagery of like
a woman that's soiled,

00:57:44.180 --> 00:57:48.050
it's a terrible, terribly horrible piece.

00:57:48.050 --> 00:57:52.040
And it is read before the
ninth day of the month of Av,

00:57:52.040 --> 00:57:53.680
which is the day of the destruction

00:57:53.680 --> 00:57:56.030
of not only the first temple,
but also the second temple.

00:57:56.030 --> 00:57:58.040
And then just as the way that
kind of like, I don't know,

00:57:58.040 --> 00:58:01.680
religious coincidence works
like many other horrible things

00:58:01.680 --> 00:58:02.840
that have ever happened
to the Jewish people

00:58:02.840 --> 00:58:04.390
have happened on the ninth day of Av.

00:58:04.390 --> 00:58:08.480
So it's kind of a national day of mourning

00:58:09.740 --> 00:58:11.240
historic and current.

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:14.810
And we read that Book of
Lamentations on this horrible day,

00:58:14.810 --> 00:58:16.380
thinking about all the terrible
things that have happened

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:17.480
to the Jews.

00:58:17.480 --> 00:58:20.330
And before we read that
Book of Lamentations,

00:58:20.330 --> 00:58:23.290
we say a blessing, at
least in my community.

00:58:23.290 --> 00:58:25.640
And we say that we are
(speaking in foreign language)

00:58:25.640 --> 00:58:27.423
we are captives of hope.

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:32.940
Like it's such an amazing phrase, captive,

00:58:32.940 --> 00:58:34.220
when you're captive to something,

00:58:34.220 --> 00:58:37.380
it's almost like you're
being held against your will.

00:58:37.380 --> 00:58:41.700
And despite yourself and there are times

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:44.620
when it's very easy for
me to feel very hopeful.

00:58:44.620 --> 00:58:47.460
I'm a generally optimistic
person, and then there are times

00:58:47.460 --> 00:58:51.290
where I feel like it's actually impossible

00:58:51.290 --> 00:58:54.830
and yet I'm a captive of hope.

00:58:54.830 --> 00:58:58.490
I think that that is what
we're called to be in a sense.

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:00.270
The text, I think, is
originally from Jeremiah,

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:05.270
but that phrase, but this
idea that we have no choice,

00:59:05.970 --> 00:59:09.730
but to be held to that
expectation and we cannot give in

00:59:09.730 --> 00:59:13.431
to the kind of the fashion
of the day to despair,

00:59:13.431 --> 00:59:15.000
you can't afford that.

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:17.790
So I think that that's my general take,

00:59:17.790 --> 00:59:19.990
sometimes it comes more
easily than others.

00:59:19.990 --> 00:59:21.560
- Yeah, I love that phrase

00:59:21.560 --> 00:59:23.670
and I'm definitely gonna
carry that with me.

00:59:23.670 --> 00:59:27.010
Out of this conversation,
hopefully keep that in my spirit,

00:59:27.010 --> 00:59:27.843
so to speak.

00:59:27.843 --> 00:59:29.420
So again, I appreciate everything.

00:59:29.420 --> 00:59:33.620
- I sense you're a kindred
captive of hope, Nii.

00:59:33.620 --> 00:59:36.003
- I will take that, I
will definitely take that.

00:59:36.003 --> 00:59:39.240
And I think that it's
true in a lot of ways.

00:59:39.240 --> 00:59:43.440
And honestly, having people
like you on this podcast

00:59:43.440 --> 00:59:45.437
has been a general reminder of that.

00:59:45.437 --> 00:59:48.250
And as people have shared
their reasons for hope

00:59:48.250 --> 00:59:50.830
and the ways that
they've seen things move,

00:59:50.830 --> 00:59:52.660
